            <ArticleTitle>In which orthodontics discovers a defender.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>662-663</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The specifics of this hassle seem to center around extraction versus nonextraction rationales of orthodontic treatment. To suggest that one must never extract teeth for orthodontic care seems as patently ridiculous as to say one must always extract teeth, yet I've nerve heard an orthodontist suggest always extract for proper treatment planning. One should be deeply suspicious of the always-never neuroses.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
